Dear Tiffany,

I am pleased to recommend Sophia for a position as a Santa’s helper. I have known Sophia for many years and have had many opportunities to observe her judgment, customer-service skills, good manners, and ability to work constructively with other people.

Sophia has an unusually effective approach to customer service. She is warm and approachable without ever being pushy. She makes people feel comfortable, treats them with respect, and has a quiet professionalism that earns trust. In a previous customer-facing position, I watched her supervisor repeatedly point to her as the example other employees should follow because of the way she treated customers.

Having worked professionally as Santa, I believe Sophia would have fit naturally into our team. She would have been welcoming families, organizing the line, talking with nervous children while they waited, communicating professionally with parents, and helping everything run smoothly without trying to become the center of attention.

One of the most important qualities in a Santa’s helper is judgment—knowing when to step in and when to step back. I remember one little girl who quietly wandered around the Santa set, gradually becoming curious. None of the helpers rushed her or interrupted. I simply waved and asked, “What’s your name?” Later, when she seemed comfortable, I asked, “What do you want for Christmas?” Afterward, her parents told me she was autistic and that this was the first year she had ever interacted with a Santa. Based on my experience with Sophia, I believe she would have shown that same patience and restraint, allowing the child to set the pace instead of trying to force the interaction.

If a child was crying, I can imagine Sophia quietly saying, “Sometimes I cry too.” That simple kind of empathy reflects the way she connects with people and helps them feel understood.

Good judgment also means recognizing when support is needed. During one busy shift, a group of obnoxious teenage boys approached Santa laughing and said, “Santa, please let me sit on your lap, it’s my lifelong dream.” I had to de-escalate the situation myself because I couldn’t get a manager’s attention. I believe Sophia would have stepped in immediately, established appropriate boundaries, and helped restore order without embarrassing anyone or escalating the encounter.

Sophia is also the kind of coworker who prepares thoroughly. Before the season even began, I believe she would have learned how the entire Santa set operated—where everything belonged, how the daily routine worked, and why things were done a certain way—so that she would be ready before the first family arrived.

As the Christmas season becomes busier, Santa inevitably becomes tired. I can easily picture Sophia noticing before anyone else and asking, “Santa, is it time for a break?” If I agreed, she would tell the waiting families, “Santa needs to go take care of the reindeer,” preserving the magic for the children while making sure I had a chance to rest and get something to drink before returning to the set.

Our Santa team also included two sixteen-year-old helpers who were just beginning their working lives. One practical challenge they ran into was figuring out how they would be paid, since minors could not be set up for direct deposit in the same way as adult employees. Sophia would have stepped in to help them navigate that process, making sure they understood what information was needed and how to complete the paperwork correctly so there were no delays or confusion. It was a small but important example of her ability to quietly solve real-world problems and support younger coworkers without making them feel overwhelmed.

What impresses me most about Sophia is that she notices what people need. She supports the people around her, exercises sound judgment, and helps an operation succeed without seeking recognition for herself. I would have been very comfortable having her as part of my Santa team, and I recommend her with confidence.

Sincerely,

Santa Alex
